      "China remains one of India's largest trading partners, and a significant number of Indian businesses — from e-commerce sellers to manufacturers sourcing components — import regularly from Chinese suppliers. Understanding the practical mechanics of this specific corridor helps avoid the common pitfalls that trip up businesses new to importing from China.\n\nOcean freight from major Chinese ports to Indian gateways like Nhava Sheva or Chennai is a well-served, high-volume route with strong carrier capacity and generally short transit times relative to other major international lanes, commonly under two weeks depending on the specific port pair and routing. Air freight is faster still and increasingly used for e-commerce and smaller commercial shipments where speed matters more than the cost premium.\n\nSupplier vetting deserves particular attention on this corridor, given the sheer scale and variety of manufacturers and trading companies operating out of China, ranging from established, quality-consistent manufacturers to less reliable intermediaries. Verifying a supplier's actual manufacturing capability — through video calls, third-party inspection services, or a trial order before committing to significant volume — is standard practice for experienced importers and worth the upfront investment for anyone sourcing from a new supplier for the first time.\n\nIndia applies duty rates that vary considerably by product category on imports from China, and certain product categories have specifically faced anti-dumping duties or other trade remedy measures over the years, reflecting periodic trade tension between the two countries around specific industries. Checking whether your specific product category currently faces any such additional duty, beyond the standard tariff, is worth doing close to the time of import rather than assuming the duty landscape from a previous shipment still applies, since this is one of the more actively shifting aspects of India-China trade.\n\nQuality control before goods leave China is considerably cheaper and easier to act on than discovering a quality issue after goods have already cleared Indian customs and been paid for in full. Third-party pre-shipment inspection services, which physically check goods against agreed specifications before they're loaded, are widely available and commonly used by experienced importers specifically for this reason — the relatively small inspection cost is generally far less than the cost of receiving and having to deal with a substandard shipment after the fact.\n\nFor businesses new to importing from China, working with a customs broker experienced specifically in China-India trade, who understands current duty treatment, common documentation issues, and the practical realities of both origin Chinese ports and destination Indian ports, considerably smooths the process compared to treating it as a generic international import. Given how large and active this trade corridor is, this kind of specific experience is relatively easy to find among Indian customs brokers and forwarders, and it's worth prioritizing over a generalist without a demonstrated track record on this specific lane.\n\nPayment terms deserve particular care when sourcing from Chinese suppliers, especially for a new relationship without an established track record. Many experienced importers use a staged payment structure — a deposit before production begins, with the balance paid only after pre-shipment inspection confirms the goods meet agreed specifications — rather than paying the full amount upfront, which provides meaningful leverage to address any quality issues discovered before the goods have already been fully paid for and shipped. Letters of credit remain a reasonable option for larger transactions with new suppliers, offering payment security similar to what's discussed in the context of first-time exporting generally.\n\nIt's also worth building awareness of Chinese public holidays, particularly the extended Lunar New Year period, when factory production and shipping activity across the country slows dramatically or stops entirely for one to two weeks, with knock-on effects on capacity and lead times in the weeks both before and after the holiday itself. Businesses that don't account for this annual disruption in their ordering and inventory planning often find themselves caught short exactly when demand doesn't pause even though Chinese production effectively does — building this predictable annual slowdown into your sourcing calendar, similar to the peak season planning discussed elsewhere, avoids an entirely foreseeable supply gap.\n\nFor businesses importing regularly from China, it's worth periodically reassessing whether your current supplier remains competitive on both price and reliability relative to alternatives, since China's manufacturing landscape shifts over time — a supplier that was highly competitive a few years ago may have been overtaken by others, and a periodic, low-pressure market check keeps your sourcing decisions current rather than anchored to a relationship formed under different market conditions.",
